We have derived improved differentiability properties for solutions to a non-autonomous obstacle problem, formulated as
  \begin{equation*}
  \min \left\{\int_{\Omega}f\left(x, Dv(x)\right)dx\,:\, v\in
  \mathcal{K}_\psi(\Omega)\right\}.
  \end{equation*} Given the growth restrictions imposed on the function $f$, with respect to the gradient variable, we find that when the obstacle function is bounded and satisfies certain regularity conditions, the partial map $x\mapsto D_\xi f(x,\xi)$ being part of the Sobolev space, leads to a corresponding increase in the differentiability of the solution, provided the partial map $x\mapsto D_\xi f(x,\xi)$ belongs to a $W^{1, p+2}$. Notably, we address subquadratic growth conditions on the function $f$, i.e., where $f(x,\xi)\approx a(x)|\xi|^p$ and $1<p<2$ and map $a$ also belongs to a Sobolev space, where $\Omega$ and $x,\xi\in \Omega$. Furthermore, we establish the differentiability of the solution when the obstacle function is part of a Sobolev space, where $\Omega$ and $x\in \Omega$. Additionally, we tackle the case of vanishing obstacle function. Moreover, we address the higher differentiability of the solution when the domain $\Omega$ is smooth and the partial map $x\mapsto D_\xi f(x,\xi)$ belongs to a Sobolev space, where $\xi\in \mathbb{R}^n$. Lastly, we provide an application of these results to the regularity of solutions to the $p$-Laplace equation, formulated as
